one of my colleagues said to me  yesterday uh  is what the government did this week  a boost to the northern powerhouse or  does it really feel like the great train  robbery to you  well i have to i laughed to myself as a  manchester united fan when you  introduced the topic  i'm not sure who's handling uh priority  matters worse the glazer family with  united or  boris and his team with this i mean  you know they're both they're both  absolutely shambolic to be honest with  you the as my  uh  ex-chancellor when i was a briefly a  minister said i think to the ft  yesterday that  who would who would have thought  announcing a package close to 100  billion pounds could go down so badly  and  i think i think there's two problems the  first one is  uh  this government  seems to be uh  completely inept to expectations  management um  you know this is a government that's  taught i think factually somewhere  between 60 and 70 times about delivering  northern powerhouse rail in full  which which is  right at the core of the whole idea of  the northern powerhouse concept which  obviously  i'm in the middle of um  and uh they they've sort of penny  pinched um  but you know if this would have been the  first time that ever made a policy  announcement about northern powerhouse  rail it might not have gone down quite  as badly as it has but  what they've managed to do is is  disappoint virtually everybody including  manchester by the way because  as much as they still got hs2 and all  the bits of northern powerhouse rail  they haven't got what they wanted at the  station either  uh and of course the key part of the  agglomeration is it's not really linking  us up to  leads properly  which was supposed to be the priority  first part  uh of northern powerhouse rail and  indeed the integrated rail plan so  you know in isolation if it would have  just appeared from nowhere it might not  have been so bad but given everything  that's followed since uh boris johnson  became prime minister  as as we can see with the reaction  across the board it's pretty  disappointing so so both so the problem  is both substance and handling and by  the way we'll come to the football  question a bit later but um  let's uh listen to what uh grant shaps  the transport secretary said or let let  me remind you what he said that um  the  government  plan would  halve journey times in some parts of the  north uh  from  manchester to leeds and so on by  upgrading surely that's a win of some  description  well you know i've spent a lot of time  on it and i  you know i keep trying to uh  chat with real uh rail engineering  experts because indeed  if the government's plan delivers six  trains an hour from leeds to manchester  in 33 minutes  uh that is pretty good  but the the problem is uh with what they  have specifically said they're going to  do  i i'm yet to find a single technical  expert that believes it's deliverable  because they've got a new new line from  leeds to huddersfield  but from huddersfield uh on to leeds  it's essentially a a planned upgrade of  the existing one and as anybody that  travels uh across the penins on that  line never mind the m62 knows  you know the the the the line doesn't  seem to be capable enough of dealing  with what it has now never mind more in  the future so  it's just  if if one could take for 100  certainty  the great powers of digitalization uh  which which is implicitly instrumental  to this new plan then it would be  fantastic but that that's sort of  assuming that the life  and delivery of any kind of  infrastructure in the uk always works so  smoothly and we know it  we know it doesn't  the people who i suppose feel most  aggrieved are uh people of bradford who  as you i think have pointed out um have  the youngest workforce uh in the uk  unfortunately that workforce now can't  really travel to the places where there  are the the jobs is that  if you like  the central problem  that with what the government's done  that you have that it's not really  joining up  the metropolitan areas of the north  i mean you know when i in the early days  of trying to get uh the northern  powerhouse the idea adopted i used to  sort of in elegantly call it manchester  leeds pool  because if you take manchester to any of  those other three cities the distance is  less than the central line tube line in  london so the whole idea is to try and  get all the people that live within that  area  uh whether they be producers or  consumers it's about eight million  people to be able to move around as  easily as people do on the london uh  transport system and to have it all  integrated  and obviously for people in bradford  they they  they've got a they've got a sort of  token gesture of electrification from  bradford to leeds but that means they've  got to go the opposite way in order to  go to manchester afterwards and  obviously that that's like going from  say i don't know  um  richmond out to out to reading before  you come back into london so obviously  for people from bradford it's  particularly unhelpful but i would add  again trevor that you know those of us  that have been following it with great  closeness for the past couple of years  they could have handled that better  because it seemed quite obvious to me  that the bradford link wouldn't happen  but yet uh they've allowed a lot of  people to think that it would uh because  it is quite the bradford bit to do  uh and have the train going through  bradford would be quite expensive but  i've i've seen since and here's another  thing to throw in given the the politics  of all of this  and interestingly how the labour  opposition is now  seemingly saying even more powerfully  than i recall them saying in the past  that they would deliver in full  including bradford the political  pressures might come back  uh and get them to rethink again i mean  the one thing is for sure with all this  never-ending nonsense surrounding  northern transport is there's always  some other idea in the next couple of  years so i i hope in this case this is  not the end of northern powerhouse rail  because  whilst there are bigger issues than  transport northern powerhouse rail is  central to the whole idea that i helped  create  well you're talking about the politics  of it and you're right most people  wherever they are aren't going to be  paying too much attention to the detail  of it but i suppose that the politics of  this week  could look like that actually this is a  government which essentially  thinks first about the south and the  north gets the leavings in the sense  london has crossrail and nothing has  been allowed to get in the way of  delivering that uh delays higher pr  higher costs  all sorts of issues and planning and so  on uh so but crossrail is going to  happen come what may but suddenly  the north buff you're not getting the  thing that as you say everybody thought  you were going to get  well let's see whether they they they  revise that because of course  the downside of very strong expectations  creation is that it does create belief  and and  what's that a spirit and a desire and a  passion  and so and that's partly what created  the victory for them in the election but  if they want to stay in power  uh they've obviously got to show some  evidence of following through  um i i would add in addition uh i i  smell that there  is been quite a bit of the hand of the  treasury playing around to try and save  a few billion here and there in the  background but again for a strategic  government that judges risk reward of  these things properly  you know from what i can see they're  basically trying to save about 4 billion  by not going all the way through to  leeds from the original  announced plan for northern powerhouse  rail and it doesn't re given the amount  of money we're talking about here that's  about 10 of what was the original total  and that that seems to me to be a bad  risk rewards judgment from people in the  treasury as well to be honest  okay i can't let you go without asking  you what might be called the manchester  city question arising out of all of this  which is that um  i  but for fans of manchester united there  is some quite good news here because uh  the high-speed line will continue to run  from the south uh to birmingham and then  on to manchester united so that huge  percentage of manchester united fans who  don't come from manchester united  they're still going to be able to get to  old trafford aren't they  they they might find out if things carry  on the way that they're going that they  don't want to go there too much anymore  manchester united needs uh  needs owners that have uh profit with  purpose rather than uh no purpose  whatsoever completely shambolic how  they're presiding over this fiasco  complete  completely shambolic

